\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Marine inertial motion estimation has a structural difficulty that is distinct
from ordinary attitude estimation.  Accelerometer bias, small attitude errors,
and low-frequency model mismatch are integrated into velocity and displacement,
while the desired signal itself is oscillatory and must not be removed by the
same mechanism that suppresses drift.  This is especially severe horizontally,
where first-order gravity leakage from tilt competes with real surge and sway.
At the same time, a deployable estimator has to operate across sea states whose
characteristic periods and acceleration levels vary by large factors.

Recent heave estimators span discrete Kalman reconstruction, adaptive IMU
filtering, nonlinear observers, and frequency-assisted approaches
\cite{Reis2023_DiscreteKalmanHeave,Lu2023_AdaptiveDelayFreeHeave,
Li2025_HeaveCKF,YurovskyKudinov2025_IMUwaves,Hope2025_SFY}.  The contribution of
this work is not the Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process itself: first-order
Gauss--Markov acceleration models are classical
\cite{UhlenbeckOrnstein1930_OU,Singer1970_ManeuverModel}.  The contribution is
to integrate such a prior into a fully specified three-dimensional quaternion
MEKF with an additional integral-displacement state, to regularize that state in
a way that remains compatible with the coupled Kalman covariance, and to build
an exogenous sea-state adaptation layer whose effect can be separated from the
core estimator by controlled ablation.

The article is organized deliberately from mathematics to engineering.  First,
Secs.~\ref{sec:state}--\ref{app:iss-stability} define the 21-state estimator,
its measurements, exact discrete translational model, Kalman updates, and the
local Live-state stability result.  The zero pseudo-measurement on the integral
state is part of that core formulation: it is a regularizer, not a physical
observation.  Only after the mathematical estimator and its stability scope are
fixed do Secs.~\ref{sec:adaptation-motivation} and~\ref{sec:adaptation} develop
the sea-state scaling arguments, measurement-only wave statistics, and the
empirically calibrated schedule used by the implementation.  Startup and
magnetic calibration are then treated as separate engineering layers, followed
by the experimental protocol and results.

\subsection{Main contributions}
The strongest contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item a 21-state left-multiplicative quaternion MEKF coupling attitude,
        gyro and residual accelerometer bias, and three exact OU-driven
        $[v,p,S,a_w]$ chains, with the integral state $S$ providing a soft
        low-frequency drift-control channel rather than a direct position
        constraint;
  \item a constructive local stability treatment of the normal Live recursion:
        finite-horizon observability of the translational and
        attitude--gyro-bias blocks, a finite-correlation residual bias model,
        bounded Riccati covariance, uniform exponential stability of the
        homogeneous error recursion, and a local ISS bound under the explicit
        bounded-schedule, reference-geometry, covariance, and no-reset
        assumptions stated in Sec.~\ref{app:iss-stability};
  \item an exogenous adaptation architecture that estimates the wave time scale
        and acceleration level from measurement-only signals, then schedules the
        OU prior and integral regularizer.  The scaling analysis explains why
        these are natural variables; the deployed law and coefficients are
        engineering choices supported by full-filter experiments rather than
        claimed as an exact optimum of the reduced analytical models;
  \item a versioned paired evaluation that tests vertical and horizontal
        displacement, attitude, bias tracking, stationary and changing seas,
        and adaptation-channel ablations.  In the current isotropic
        configuration OU--III improves the paired mean 3-D displacement RMS
        relative to matched OU--II in all four stationary JONSWAP cases and the
        controlled transition, while the adaptation ablation identifies the
        integral-regularization channel as the principal source of the adaptive
        benefit.
\end{itemize}

The scope remains narrower than the title ``navigation'' can suggest.  The
reported displacement $\vct p$ is bounded wave-induced motion about a local
reference, not geodetic position.  GNSS aiding and a separated absolute
trajectory are companion designs, not evaluated results.  The evidence in this
paper is primarily synthetic; the ESP32--S3 exercise establishes source
portability but not timing, power, memory margin, or externally referenced
accuracy.  The residual accelerometer-bias state is centered on a deterministic
temperature model, but temperature is not swept in the scored experiments, so
thermal-calibration performance is not claimed.  Startup resets, magnetic
reference changes, and other hybrid reconfiguration events are outside the
Live-state ISS theorem and are evaluated separately rather than hidden inside
its assumptions.
